AEB — Agentic Escalation Benchmark: Measuring When AI Agents Know They
Shouldn't Act Alone
Ganesh Kothule  |  Mercor Research Fellowship Proposal
1. Motivation / Problem
Agent evaluation today overwhelmingly measures task completion: did the agent finish the workflow, book the flight, resolve the ticket. This is
the wrong axis to optimize alone. Deployed agents in professional domains — finance, operations, customer recovery — regularly face
situations where the correct behavior is not to act independently: information is missing, the action is irreversible, or the case sits outside the
agent's authorized scope. An agent that always attempts the task can score well on completion benchmarks while being dangerously
over-autonomous in production. Conversely, an overly cautious agent that escalates everything is unusable.
2. Research Question
In ambiguous, partially-specified operational tasks, how reliably do LLM agents choose the correct response mode — act, ask a clarifying
question, escalate to a human, or stop — and under what conditions do they systematically over-act or under-act?
3. Proposed Benchmark / Methodology
Agent benchmarks, including long-horizon agentic suites like APEX-Agents, primarily grade whether the task was completed, and
safety-focused agent evaluations typically test refusal of clearly harmful requests. AEB targets the middle case that both miss: a request that is
plausible and not overtly harmful, but where the agent still lacks sufficient information or authority to act on it alone. This case fails silently — a
model that quietly exceeds its authority on a reasonable-looking request is invisible to a refusal benchmark and rewarded by a completion
benchmark.
I propose building AEB, a benchmark of 40–60 hand-designed scenarios across four categories: missing-information ambiguity,
policy-boundary edge cases (an amount at or near an approval threshold), conflicting-signal situations (available data points support two
different actions), and irreversible-action scenarios (the action, once taken, cannot be undone or cheaply corrected, e.g. a permanent account
closure vs. a reversible temporary hold). Each scenario includes a short operational context, an explicit policy constraint, and mocked tools
implemented as scripted function stubs — no live systems, so the benchmark isolates decision judgment from tool-execution reliability. Each
task carries a gold-label decision mode, with some tasks legitimately supporting more than one acceptable mode.
Given the initial dataset is single-author, I will run a pilot labeling pass on a 15-task subset with a second rater before finalizing gold labels, and
revise the rubric or task wording where agreement is low before writing the remaining tasks.
4. Evaluation Framework
Each model output is scored on two axes: a 4-way decision-mode classification against the gold label, and a 1–5 justification-quality score for
the stated reasoning. I will hand-grade a sample of outputs myself and separately score the same sample with an LLM-as-judge, then report
agreement using percent agreement and Cohen's kappa. This tests where automated grading diverges from human judgment on agentic
decisions specifically, a less-studied case than single-turn response grading. From the graded outputs I will also build a small error taxonomy
describing recurring failure patterns.
Given the sample size, I will treat cross-model differences below roughly 10 percentage points as inconclusive rather than a finding, and report
confidence intervals via bootstrap resampling rather than point estimates alone.
5. Experiments
Three planned experiments: cross-model comparison across 3–5 publicly accessible models, reporting decision accuracy, over-autonomy
rate, and under-autonomy rate per task category; a policy-presence ablation, comparing behavior with and without the explicit policy constraint
in the prompt, to test whether models respect stated rules more than inferred risk; and an autonomy-framing ablation, comparing
fully-autonomous framing against neutral framing. Two baselines, always-act and always-escalate, contextualize how much real headroom
exists above trivial strategies. Token cost per task will be logged to report accuracy-per-dollar as a secondary result.
6. Expected Contribution
The core output is a reusable dataset and scoring methodology for measuring escalation judgment as its own axis, independent of task
completion, along with an open scoring script and a documented failure taxonomy. I expect directional gaps between models on
ambiguity-driven categories, and lower human-LLM judge agreement on justification quality than on decision-mode labeling, since a label is
either right or wrong against the gold set while judging the quality of stated reasoning is inherently more subjective, even between two careful
human graders. At 40–60 tasks and largely single-annotator labels, this supports methodology and directional findings, not definitive model
rankings — a limitation the pilot rating pass and mentor input are meant to partly address as the task set grows. With extension, this could
grow into a public leaderboard or a task category within an existing agentic benchmark suite.
7. Why Mercor / Why Me
This proposal comes directly from two concrete experiences. At Ethara AI, my work was data annotation, response labeling, response ranking,
error identification, and structured human feedback for LLM post-training — the exact skill set this benchmark's rubric design and
human-vs-judge comparison require. Separately, building REVIVE, an AI-assisted revenue recovery workflow, required me to design explicit
escalation conditions rather than leave action fully to model judgment — for example, routing any recovery action above a configured amount
threshold, or any case where automated retries fail without a diagnosable cause, to human review before further action, with the decision
logged to an audit trail. Designing that layer made clear how little existing evaluation tells you about whether a model actually respects
constraints like these when it counts. Mercor is the right environment because APEX treats evaluation as a first-class research discipline with
direct exposure to real professional-domain agentic tasks — exactly where escalation judgment matters most, and where I can get rigorous
feedback on rubric design and grading methodology from a team that does this at scale.
